#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447090} :activity/id #uuid "dbc6f2c2-31aa-5586-9a34-11c5fa68bf80" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050805 :topic/id #uuid "6834e4fa-5e01-5d57-8ef2-14960e40b519" :topic/math-academy-id 882} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447086 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088390 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b6a1591-a009-5bfe-b387-44fc5cfadb97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}+5x{e}^{-2x}+2{x}^{2}{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447060} {:answer/id #uuid "21e3d796-7bfd-5114-9884-3ef0cfd6184c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}+2{e}^{2x}+3x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447063} {:answer/id #uuid "46101c72-226b-5da1-b2e4-226aecaaec36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-2x}-5x{e}^{-2x}+{x}^{2}{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447068} {:answer/id #uuid "4b2ee06b-84db-5073-917f-4d881e95921d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=3{e}^{-2x}+x{e}^{-2x}-{x}^{2}{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447069} {:answer/id #uuid "a91fcab6-7786-522f-87d3-18ec395fe1a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}+6{e}^{2x}+x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447073}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447060} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fd03cd6-46a9-5261-88f7-5ff6d57c6f63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086993}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "46b48c49-0bf2-5056-8d2c-a310fc997308" :question/math-academy-id "q-8104" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+4y=4{e}^{-2x},\\,y(0)=1,\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(0)=3.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+4y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-2$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x}+Bx{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $4{e}^{-2x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $-2$, which coincides with the double root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =\\alpha (2x-2{x}^{2}){e}^{-2x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (2-8x+4{x}^{2}){e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (2-8x+4{x}^{2}){e}^{-2x}+4\\alpha (2x-2{x}^{2}){e}^{-2x}+4\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{-2x} & =4{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha (2-8x+4{x}^{2}+8x-8{x}^{2}+4{x}^{2}){e}^{-2x} & =4{e}^{-2x} \\\\ 2\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =4{e}^{-2x} \\\\ 2\\alpha  & =4 \\\\ \\alpha  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2{x}^{2}{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+Bx{e}^{-2x}+2{x}^{2}{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nLet us find constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=3.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-2x}-2Bx{e}^{-2x}+4x{e}^{-2x}-4{x}^{2}{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=3$ into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2A+B & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=1$ and $B=5.$\n\nWe substitute this into the general particular integral and find that the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-2x}+5x{e}^{-2x}+2{x}^{2}{e}^{-2x}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "20c8b2d2-f5dc-5010-aeca-b21664e05b1d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447088}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447087 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447085 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56d16767-17b6-5010-8451-1283b1734542" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=\\frac{3}{5}x{e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447070} {:answer/id #uuid "58c062dd-8a45-5d17-bfc5-9f64f86a9123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=2{e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447071} {:answer/id #uuid "a3baac3d-682d-560f-9520-e7d20861ac53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=\\frac{2}{7}x{e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447072} {:answer/id #uuid "c5846cd6-8be1-5e0d-a5c7-f5e507118c76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=2x{e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447075} {:answer/id #uuid "d960ac68-9a21-53d4-8e94-c4e61b477226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=\\frac{3}{5}{e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447077}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447072} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e44c597-8e44-5c20-ae30-4c0ebd006187" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e0127162-0b7c-5790-9745-aeb2f8fdc336" :question/math-academy-id "q-64224" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-10y=2{e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{5x}+B{e}^{-2x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "Since the complementary function is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{5x}+B{e}^{-2x},$ the roots of the auxiliary equation are $\\lambda =5$ and $\\lambda =-2.$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $2{e}^{5x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $5$, which coincides with one of the roots of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha (5x+1){e}^{5x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (25x+10){e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p}$, ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (25x+10){e}^{5x}-3(\\alpha (5x+1){e}^{5x})-10(\\alpha x{e}^{5x}) & =2{e}^{5x} \\\\ (25x+10-15x-3-10x)\\alpha {e}^{5x} & =2{e}^{5x} \\\\ 7\\alpha {e}^{5x} & =2{e}^{5x} \\\\ 7\\alpha  & =2 \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{2}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{2}{7}x{e}^{5x}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4e7b9fd0-5a09-5a0f-8a80-f1f2ee4aff2e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447089}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447088 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351213 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2840b6e1-4742-5106-a409-f9ebe1f9eac8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-3x+\\pi /2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+3{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348796} {:answer/id #uuid "61564fd4-c441-55b7-b77e-31271079268c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349338} {:answer/id #uuid "9f3a3285-61f3-56fc-9cc1-46aaddd3d1a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=4{e}^{-3x+\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349867} {:answer/id #uuid "a4103855-a4a3-555b-9430-dd5e6f6b1de5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=4{e}^{-3x+\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-3{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349913} {:answer/id #uuid "e98f3cbc-9be5-5a42-bc43-ea6509fc2b68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=4{e}^{-x}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-3{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350494}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349913} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ee59664-5ed9-57ac-9ca5-6c88f3ce2892" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350732}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0a7f18b4-bfd2-5978-b5ec-b53743924e99" :question/math-academy-id "q-8108" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+10y=-6{e}^{-2x},\\,y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={e}^{-\\pi },\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-2{e}^{-\\pi }.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+10y=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +10=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +10 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9)-9+10 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda +3 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-3\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c}(x) & ={e}^{-3x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $-6{e}^{-2x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $-2$, which is not the root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\alpha {e}^{-2x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=4\\alpha {e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha {e}^{-2x}+6(-2\\alpha {e}^{-2x})+10(\\alpha {e}^{-2x}) & =-6{e}^{-2x} \\\\ (4-12+10)\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =-6{e}^{-2x} \\\\ 2\\alpha  & =-6 \\\\ \\alpha  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-3{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-3x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-3{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $P$ and $Q.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={e}^{-\\pi }$ into the general solution gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q{e}^{-3\\pi /2}-3{e}^{-\\pi } & ={e}^{-\\pi } \\\\ Q{e}^{-3\\pi /2} & =4{e}^{-\\pi } \\\\ Q & =4{e}^{\\pi /2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-2{e}^{-\\pi }.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(Q-3P){e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-(P+3Q){e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+6{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-2{e}^{-\\pi }$ into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(P+3Q){e}^{-3\\pi /2}+6{e}^{-\\pi } & =-2{e}^{-\\pi } \\\\ -(P+12{e}^{\\pi /2}){e}^{-3\\pi /2} & =-8{e}^{-\\pi } \\\\ -P{e}^{-3\\pi /2}-12{e}^{-\\pi } & =-8{e}^{-\\pi } \\\\ -P{e}^{-3\\pi /2} & =4{e}^{-\\pi } \\\\ P & =-4{e}^{\\pi /2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $P=-4{e}^{\\pi /2}$ and $Q=4{e}^{\\pi /2}.$\n\nWe substitute this into the general particular integral and find that the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(-4{e}^{\\pi /2}){e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(4{e}^{\\pi /2}){e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3{e}^{-2x} \\\\ y & =4{e}^{-3x+\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-3{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "61e97ff2-aa89-5a93-8e57-5f7239d24440" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447087}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447089 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447083 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e7337fc-999b-5131-8631-92e01aaf02d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{x}-10x{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447061} {:answer/id #uuid "1ee3f409-fe04-5e2e-894e-54a520539c4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}+5{x}^{2}{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447062} {:answer/id #uuid "3f665c15-7126-5b79-9c96-ec96bd4f59b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}-2{x}^{2}{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447067} {:answer/id #uuid "fab50143-e491-524f-9c66-773e85c59b09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{x}+5x{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447078} {:answer/id #uuid "fe3eda2e-b500-56d2-8271-d1e7d47bb69b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}+10{x}^{2}{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447079}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447062} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1a20dc0-72d2-5e4c-9083-07d17fe06487" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447081}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "15f1d281-e0ee-5d82-9eed-6ca553720ad8" :question/math-academy-id "q-93690" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=10{e}^{-x}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-1$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $10{e}^{-x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $-1$, which coincides with the double root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =\\alpha (2x-{x}^{2}){e}^{-x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (2-4x+{x}^{2}){e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (2-4x+{x}^{2}){e}^{-x}+2\\alpha (2x-{x}^{2}){e}^{-x}+\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{-x} & =10{e}^{-x} \\\\ \\alpha (2-4x+{x}^{2}+4x-2{x}^{2}+{x}^{2}){e}^{-x} & =10{e}^{-x} \\\\ 2\\alpha {e}^{-x} & =10{e}^{-x} \\\\ 2\\alpha  & =10 \\\\ \\alpha  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5{x}^{2}{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}+5{x}^{2}{e}^{-x}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c35c9e16-3936-5c81-a3e3-0dadefd5a147"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447090 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447084 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "252d5d3b-ac6a-5c53-983a-089a39826fc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-3x}-x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447064} {:answer/id #uuid "3aab89b2-a38d-527c-af67-524c43eceb4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}+{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447065} {:answer/id #uuid "3b8351e0-da01-5550-9f08-e306a98b69ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{-3x}+3{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447066} {:answer/id #uuid "b7065681-8e41-5f70-ba3f-bbba136f4621" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-3x}+3x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447074} {:answer/id #uuid "d6a92f44-ceec-54d5-acd4-9f77e7f779be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-3x}+6x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447076}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447074} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2016737-56b8-5480-9925-df39e2b6f1f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447082}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "42225f24-8460-51a6-92c2-adb948ec3f96" :question/math-academy-id "q-8035" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y=3{e}^{-2x}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda +3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-2$ or $\\lambda =-3$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $3{e}^{-2x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $-2$, which coincides with one of the roots of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha (1-2x){e}^{-2x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=4\\alpha (x-1){e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha (x-1){e}^{-2x}+5(\\alpha (1-2x){e}^{-2x})+6(\\alpha x{e}^{-2x}) & =3{e}^{-2x} \\\\ (4x-4+5-10x+6x)\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =3{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =3{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3x{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-3x}+3x{e}^{-2x}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "de27df4a-8842-5af7-83c2-9b1a4d4b9104" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447086}}] :activity/title "Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODEs With Exponential Forcing" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447059}]}
